---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import XYProblemDiagram from "../../components/XYProblemDiagram.tsx";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="The XY Problem - AI Training Guide">
  <PageShell chapterId="task-breakdowns" subChapterId="the-xy-problem">
    <div class="prose space-y-6">
      <p>
        You have problem X. You decide solution Y will fix it. You get stuck
        on Y and ask for help with Y. But Y was never the right approach, and
        now you've spent time getting a perfect answer to the wrong question.
        This is the XY Problem.
      </p>

      <p>
        The term comes from tech support forums, where people would ask "How
        do I get the last three characters of a filename?" when their actual
        problem was "How do I get a file's extension?" The attempted solution
        (substring parsing) masked the real question, and the help they
        received solved the wrong thing.
      </p>

      <XYProblemDiagram client:visible />

      <h3>Try It Yourself</h3>

      <p>
        Open your AI tool of choice and ask it this exact question:
      </p>

      <Callout variant="example" title="Prompt">
        <p class="italic">
          "How can I echo the last three characters in a filename?"
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <p>
        Here is the actual response from Gemini when asked this question:
      </p>

      <div
        class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4 my-8 text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed space-y-3"
      >
        <p class="font-medium text-stone-800">
          Extracting the last three characters of a filename is a classic task
          in shell scripting. Depending on which tool you prefer, there are a
          few elegant ways to handle it.
        </p>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs bg-stone-100 rounded px-3 py-2">
          echo "$&#123;FILE: -3&#125;"
        </p>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs bg-stone-100 rounded px-3 py-2">
          echo "$FILE" | rev | cut -c 1-3 | rev
        </p>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs bg-stone-100 rounded px-3 py-2">
          echo "$FILE" | sed 's/.*\(...\)/\1/'
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs text-stone-500 italic">
          ...followed by a comparison table of all three methods, and then
          buried at the very bottom:
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs text-stone-600 border-l-2 border-stone-300 pl-3">
          <strong>Important Note:</strong> If your goal is specifically to get
          the <strong>file extension</strong> (like .jpg), keep in mind that
          extensions can be different lengths (e.g., .py vs .html). In that
          case, using <code>$&#123;FILE##*.&#125;</code> is usually safer as
          it captures everything after the last dot.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p>
        Three detailed solutions to the wrong problem. The AI did include a
        note about file extensions at the very bottom, but only as an
        afterthought after thoroughly answering the question as asked. It
        never stopped to ask <em>why</em> you want the last three characters.
        A human more likely would have.
      </p>

      <p>
        Now try asking: <em>"How do I get a file's extension?"</em> and
        compare the answers. The second prompt leads to a better
        solution because it describes the actual problem instead of an
        assumed approach and allows for extensions to be different lengths.
      </p>

      <h3>People Have This Problem Too</h3>

      <p>
        The XY Problem has existed long before AI. People do this to each other all
        the time. You have probably been on both sides of it: asking a
        coworker how to do something specific and them asking, "Wait, why are
        you doing it that way?"
      </p>

      <p>
        The difference is that people sometimes catch it. A coworker who
        shares context about your project might say "Hold on, what are you
        actually trying to do?" They can recognize when a question feels off
        because they understand your goals and may have encountered something similar before.
      </p>

      <p>
        This does not always get caught, which is why you can often find overly verbose and complex solutions to problems that could be solved in a simpler way in your projects.
      </p>

      <Callout variant="warning" title="This job stays with you">
        <p>
          Remember that the AI is like a new employee on their first day.
          It does not know your project's history, your team's goals, or
          why past decisions were made. A senior colleague might push back
          on your approach because they have that context. The AI does not
          even know what to push back on. You are the one who needs to step
          back and check whether you are solving the right problem and describe it correctly.
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <h3>Self Check</h3>

      <p>
        Before you send a prompt to an AI tool, ask yourself one question:
      </p>

      <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-stone-300 pl-4 py-2 my-6 text-stone-600 italic">
        "Am I describing the problem I have, or the solution I am trying?"
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        If the answer is "the solution," rewrite the prompt to lead with the
        problem. You should still mention the approach you want to take, but let the AI see the full picture.
      </p>

      <Callout variant="tip" title="Reframe your prompts">
        <p class="mb-2">
          <strong>Before (solution-focused):</strong>
          <span class="italic"> "How do I parse a CSV file in Python and convert it to JSON then send it to an API?"</span>
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>After (problem-focused):</strong>{" "}
          <span class="italic">
            "I have transaction data in a CSV file that needs to go into our
            API. What's the best approach?"
          </span>
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-xs opacity-80">
          The AI might suggest building a pipeline that goes direct from CSV to API instead of going through a JSON intermediate.
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <h3>When You Catch Yourself</h3>

      <p>
        If you realize mid-conversation that you have been going down a Y
        path, you have a few options:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2">
        <li>
          <strong class="text-stone-800">Ask about the approach.</strong> Tell
          the AI: "Let me step back. The actual problem I am trying to solve
          is [X]. Is this the correct approach, or is there another way to do this?" AI has no ego and no sunk
          cost, you can use this to pivot to a different approach.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-stone-800">Start a fresh chat.</strong> If you
          have been in the middle of Y for a while, and you have rethought the problem and want to start again, the context window is now loaded
          with Y-related discussion. That can bias the AI's suggestions even
          after you reframe. A new conversation can be your best bet; reset and start again.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <Callout variant="info" title="Context window bias">
        <p>
          If your current chat has a long history of discussing solution Y,
          starting fresh is often better than trying to redirect. The AI's
          responses are influenced by everything in the conversation so far.
          A clean context lets it approach problem X without anchoring on
          your previous direction.
        </p>
      </Callout>

      <p>
        This mindset applies to every phase covered in this training. Whether
        you are gathering requirements, breaking down tasks, writing code, or
        debugging, leading with the problem instead of your assumed solution
        will consistently get better results from AI tools.
      </p>
    </div>
  </PageShell>
</MainLayout>
